WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:24.800
In Neil Gaiman’s run of The Sandman, the character&nbsp;
Death famously says, “When the first living thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:34.800
existed, I was there waiting. When the last&nbsp;
living thing dies, my job will be finished.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:43.840
I’ll put the chairs on the tables, turn off&nbsp;
the lights, and lock the universe behind me.”&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.960
And when that happens, the last creature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.840
Death will see through the door will&nbsp;
likely be—as the first one was—a microbe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:01.680
Their large numbers certainly stack the&nbsp;
odds in their favor. But it doesn’t hurt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:09.280
that many microbes have their own ways of&nbsp;
evading what we have deemed the inevitable.&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:16.800
Defying death is not as clear-cut as it&nbsp;
might initially seem. What we define as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:24.240
immortality depends a bit on what you think it&nbsp;
means to die—whether it’s the loss of your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:30.640
individual self, or the loss of&nbsp;
something that has come to exceed you.&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:38.960
It also depends on what you think it means&nbsp;
to survive. Immortality is not a guarantee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:45.600
of the microcosmos. There are predators to&nbsp;
contend with, and many simple forces of nature.&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:54.480
The tardigrade is famously resilient to these&nbsp;
forces, able to withstand extremes of temperature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:02.480
radiation, and pressure. They can survive these&nbsp;
conditions by entering cryptobiosis, desiccating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:09.760
their bodies and slowing down their metabolic&nbsp;
rate as they become a dried out ball called a tun.&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.800
The tun state protects the tardigrade and lets&nbsp;
them wait out the worst for long periods of time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:24.320
And that’s part of the legend of the invincible&nbsp;
tardigrade, driving our speculation that they will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:30.720
outlive us through any apocalypse. Researchers&nbsp;
have even predicted that tardigrades could survive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:36.000
astronomical catastrophes like an&nbsp;
asteroid crashing into our planet.&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:44.160
But invincibility is not the same as immortality.&nbsp;
Tuns have been revived after 30 years of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:51.680
cryptobiosis. But if we exclude the time spent&nbsp;
in cryptobiosis, the lifespan of most tardigrade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:58.880
species ranges from a few months to a few years.
When these tardigrades are in their tun,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.880
they’re not just protected from the&nbsp;
elements. They’re also protected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:12.000
from senescence, the thing that we call aging.
Senescence is a combination of many deteriorations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:20.240
both within cells and across them. Cells acquire&nbsp;
damage, and with time, that damage accumulates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:27.680
and accumulates. Our bodies experience that in the&nbsp;
decline of different physiological functions, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:35.200
it connects to an increased mortality as we age.
When not in a tun, even the mighty tardigrade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:45.840
experiences senescence. But we know of&nbsp;
at least one organism that does not.&nbsp;

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.920
Scientists studying hydra over several&nbsp;
years found that the mortality rates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.680
over those years were quite low. But&nbsp;
even more striking, the hydra showed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.840
no evidence of aging. You might even say&nbsp;
that the hydra remained forever young.&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:14.880
They do this by replacing their cells every 20&nbsp;
days, relying on three core populations of stem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:22.720
cells that are themselves continuously and&nbsp;
indefinitely self-renewing. These stem cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:28.640
allow the hydra to reproduce asexually by&nbsp;
budding off, and they’re also behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:35.520
hydra’s legendary powers of regeneration.
And these stem cells make the hydra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:42.000
biologically immortal. Because its cells are&nbsp;
always being replaced, the hydra is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:47.440
avoid the accumulation of damage that&nbsp;
other multicellular organisms experience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:56.560
The hydra doesn’t age. And so until another&nbsp;
force or predator intervenes, it doesn’t die.&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:04.560
So we have one definition of immortality that&nbsp;
depends on aging in multicellular organisms, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:12.480
how does that apply to single-celled creatures&nbsp;
that reproduce asexually? What does death mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:20.320
to a single cell that divides and divides and&nbsp;
divides, creating a legacy that is recursively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:28.080
itself. Is it enough to just be one of thousands&nbsp;
(or tens of thousands) of clones to ensure that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:34.880
even if you die, you still, in essence, live?
In the case of the paramecium, no.&nbsp;

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:43.600
As each paramecium divides, it ages. And it will&nbsp;
continue to do so with each division. With damage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.960
accumulating in its DNA, the paramecium will&nbsp;
start to divide more slowly, and eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:57.680
the clonal daughter cells will die off.
However, they can avoid this fate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:05.760
through conjugation, the protist equivalent of&nbsp;
sex. This act is its own sort of rejuvenation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:11.680
rewinding the clock for the paramecium.
Scientists have argued that senescence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:18.960
originated with protists like the paramecium.&nbsp;
But there are signs that some bacteria experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:27.120
aging too. They just forestall the effects of an&nbsp;
aging population by dividing asymmetrically. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:35.200
each split, one daughter cell will acquire more&nbsp;
damage than the other. And so with each division,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:42.560
one cell will rejuvenate while the other&nbsp;
will age. The longevity of the group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:48.880
is thus built on creating differences&nbsp;
between the seemingly identical individuals.&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:56.320
How does this fit in with our vision of&nbsp;
immortality? It’s hard to know. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:02.320
the question of whether we should consider&nbsp;
a flask of bacteria immortal starts to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:09.680
like a matter of semantics. It’s informed as much&nbsp;
by personal and philosophical ideas as it is by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.840
biological ones. But of course, some of the most&nbsp;
interesting questions of science and philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:23.280
boil down to issues of semantics. These aren’t&nbsp;
just questions of how the world exists, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:30.080
of how we see the world, how we describe&nbsp;
it to each other, how we imagine it.&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:36.160
For millennia, we’ve been constructing&nbsp;
immortals as part of our religions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:43.760
and myths to understand the world around&nbsp;
us. In the stories we tell, these immortals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:51.360
dictate our lives in invisible ways, and&nbsp;
they defy the thing we do not want to face.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:58.800
But microbes don’t have the same grandiosity as&nbsp;
the mythical figures we create. They don’t need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:04.640
to. The hydra may be biologically immortal,&nbsp;
but at the end of the day, it’s a tube with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:12.160
arms. And a lack of senescence aside, if a&nbsp;
predator is able to make the hydra its prey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:20.240
the hydra is still quite capable of dying.
Thank you for coming on this journey with us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:26.880
as we explore the unseen world that surrounds&nbsp;
us. And thank you especially to all these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:32.000
people. Our patrons on Patreon who make&nbsp;
it possible for us to make this show.&nbsp;

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:36.560
If you want to see more from our&nbsp;
master of microscopes james Weiss,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.560 --> 00:08:41.120
